   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In in Nov-Dec 2022 Business Botswana (BB) conducted an assessment of the environment for the uptake Renewable Energy (RE) in Botswana using a combination of data collection, online survey, key informant interviews, focused group discussions. To ensure the credibility and relevance of the findings, BB organized a panel discussion in Feb 2023 bringing together development partners, government officials and policy makers, business representatives and academia. BB used the findings and recommendations from the study to advocate for a transition towards renewable energy. The assessment was deemed necessary due to the paradoxical situation in Botswana where abundant sunshine and wind resources coexist, with a notably low adoption of renewable energy. Furthermore, the progress of the Botswana Energy Master plan, despite its acknowledged potential to address energy security and climate change issues, had come to a standstill.

Concurrently, in Jan 2023, BB organised a consultative workshop  aimed at presenting its recommendations to the government and other stakeholders. The objective was to secure their endorsement of the proposed policy reforms and to establish on a nation-wide implementation plan. The engagement yielded tangible results, with the Government and stakeholders committing to several urgent priority reforms. These commitments encompassed the development of a dedicated RE policy and a regulatory framework, the introduction of investment incentives to attract investors in RE and the prioritization of programs supporting students specialising in RE fields. 

In alignment with the outcomes of the consultative workshop,  BB shared its final report with the relevant ministries for implementation as well as with its sector leaders to enable them to follow up implementation with the ministries during their routine Sector Level Consultative Council Meetings, thereby fostering a sustained dialogue for progress. With the RBSA funding, the ILO supported BB to undertake the assessment on the enabling environment for the adoption of renewable energy in Botswana. The ILO developed the scope of work for the assessment, provided technical guidance on the methodology of the assessment itself, and reviewed the report before it was presented at a consultative workshop in Jan 2023 and validation by all the stakeholders in Feb 2023.  The ILO provided further support by engaging the UN agencies in the country by mobilising their support to the project and facilitating their participation in the panel discussion that was held in Feb 2023. The ILO provided support by way of quality oversight in the formulation of BB&#x2019;s final report on RE. Importantly, the ILO built the capacity of BB by working closely with the nominated staff and the experts who undertook the research to package the information for evidence-based advocacy by identifying the relevant ministries for specific implementation of the recommended reforms. Through this process, the ILO guided BB on how to communicate the results of the study to the policy makers and to their wider membership through their newsletter and recently revamped web site.</narrative>
   </description>
